#{[17592186233393 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233393 :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)=2,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-1)}g(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186233414 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233415 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233415 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right).$\n\n![](images/72/72a0459ee5db59562a19961d0304785fb8d46db8be24ad7839d4b8690b0dc835.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases to $-\\infty $, the graph oscillates between the values $y=-6$ and $y=6$ indefinitely. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)=DNE.\n$$"] [17592186233417 :question/problem "![](images/11/1186087a359b3065400101c7f436c3e885bf937477bdd9f73fbdaab6771aa48a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(h)$ is shown above, where\n\n$$\ng(h)=\\frac{6}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +\\frac{h}{3}).\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, what is $\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)?$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233417 :question/worked-solution "The graph shows that as we approach $h=0$ from the left, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, when we approach $h=0$ from the right, the $y$ value approaches $-2.$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(h)=-2.\n$$\n\nSince both the left and right-hand limits exist and are equal to $-2,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186233418 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(3\\pi )^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233419 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233420 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\,2}\\frac{(x-2)f(x)}{{x}^{2}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)$"] [17592186233421 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}f(x)}{(2x+3)^{2}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233443 :question/problem "Calculate $L=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{n}}{(1+n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?"] [17592186233471 :question/problem "The broker predicts that, in the future, the stock will have an overall behavior similar to the function $\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}.$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[3]{x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233471 :question/worked-solution "Let $k(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=k(x):$\n\n![](images/9b/9b81a63c0681b10343cf600cdfc17636c797aeb9b15920da4368d26643827ab5.png)\n\nThe graph shows that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }k(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233491 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{t\\to \\pi /6}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}"] [17592186233492 :question/problem "$\\lim_{t\\to {\\pi }^{-}}v(t)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}"] [17592186233507 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}(x)).$"] [17592186233508 :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_{x\\to \\,5}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}(x)-5}{x}?$"] [17592186233510 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1/2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=\n$$"] [17592186233545 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233546 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}.$  If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{2}}{f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233547 :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{'}(3)=\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{f(x)-f(3)}{x-3}=\\lim_{x\\to 3}(-x-\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$).$"] [17592186233548 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 3}(-x-1)=${{field-1}}"] [17592186233549 :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(\\,2\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,h\\,).$"] [17592186233550 :question/problem "${f}^{'}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(2-2x-h)=${{field-1}}"] [17592186233586 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-2)}(\\sqrt{x+2}+1).$"] [17592186233588 :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e8383b56998815d4c981e72c946fdbf8de77ece15e52ca2ed5c7371c8f2c8af.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to 3}{\\left(\\frac{4f(x)-12}{{x}^{2}-2x+2}\\right)}^{2}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above."] [17592186233758 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(-8{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186233759 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(-3{x}^{8}-6{x}^{4}+3{x}^{2}-15)=${{field-1}}"] [17592186233760 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{2x+8}{\\sqrt[3]{\\,3{x}^{4}+5\\,}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233761 :question/problem "If $p(x)=q(x)=-2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{4}(x)\\cdot {q}^{3}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233762 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{log}_{6}(4x-2)}{x+3}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233763 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233763 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{3}(-4x).$\n\n![](images/bb/bb8d326b427d481c69ad021a831efadf77cb90540dd79d0c3edd29f68cae41ec.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\ge 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x<0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{3}(-4x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233764 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}(2x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233766 :question/problem "Given that $\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\sqrt{5x-m}=\\sqrt{5},$ what is the value of constant $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233779 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x+1}{3{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+4}=\n$$"] [17592186233780 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$"] [17592186233800 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186233800 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={e}^{\\,{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{\\,1+4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"] [17592186233858 :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{-6}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},$ evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233859 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233859 :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6({x}^{5}-4)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{P(x)}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{5}-4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"] [17592186233860 :question/problem "![](images/26/26e2e4a51d40e9ed8e6a8fab342935e944e750587534c7cedc1855cbfddfbfc5.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}+5x-6}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233861 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233861 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+4}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $4$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x+4}.$\n\n![](images/77/77080e0c2085e5678929e33f8e0588a3639bfc11b3b629dc63310acbeba6ab57.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-4)^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+4}=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233862 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233862 :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}$ as follows:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}.$\n\n- Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3231fdde89ea829347de177152ee793203612d5cffd074e0b2738389a2581f17.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233868 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{-5{x}^{9}}{2}\\right)=${{field-1}}"] [17592186233869 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\,\\lim_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233870 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{5}^{x}-1-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233871 :question/problem "If $f(x)=3{x}^{9}$ and $g(x)=-2{x}^{-11}$, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\cdot g(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233873 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{-(x-3)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233873 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{-(x-3)}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/3f/3fb10f30533c2d1390085223e1ea78ea7d23e723bbea0550d7d236368adcfa82.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233874 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(\\sqrt[3]{8(x-1)}+2)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233875 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{11-7x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233875 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{11-7x}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/63/63ea04a354807ffcd7a6b772b78f9208d35a30252ee79539f8b49ac39e02da7e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233876 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\sqrt[3]{5x-4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233876 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{5x-4}.$ Let's sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/1e/1e154aca18a2f40e9076e617b15042c13739f87f9e26e12259c7d1d71f70aef3.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233877 :question/problem "![](images/14/14340a0938b61ef8c71a687bb280f601ade6aa062d6d4dd87d2786113692fac1.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(f(x)+2)^{4}}{(f(x)-5)^{3}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233878 :question/problem "![](images/a0/a086a1f616aee596a021ee4ef6bdc8723309cf8959719e9f55d6f20b845bd760.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the function $f(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -2}({x}^{2}+3)^{2}(5-f(x))^{3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233879 :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of the limit.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 25}{x}^{-1/2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233880 :question/problem "![](images/82/82d5db4c21b908542c17e872bb23db641cd4f66a30735b98f38ca2f3b0f545ea.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\sqrt{\\frac{(x+3)^{3}-f(x)\\,g(x)}{\\,2f(x)+g(x)\\,}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233881 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}(\\operatorname{csc}(3x)+6x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233883 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{2x}-2}{x-2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233884 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3x+25}}{x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233885 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x-2}-\\sqrt{x+2}}{x-2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233886 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{\\sqrt{x+1}-2}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233887 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}(2x){e}^{-3(x+2)})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233888 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233888 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(2x)}{{e}^{3x}}-3)=0-3=-3.\n$$"] [17592186233890 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233890 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x):$ \n\n![](images/ce/ce2078de99b2c037b0ab6d5c5dcd4dab1e8aa7005417d87ca83176bf197fd5dc.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}(5-x))=\\frac{8}{\\pi }\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=4.\n$$"] [17592186233892 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}sin\\,(\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233893 :question/problem "If $g(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $g(3)=16,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(4g(x)-3x\\sqrt{g(3)})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233894 :question/problem "Find the left- and right-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ of the piecewise function $f(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-\\pi +\\sqrt{3})^{2} & \\text{ for }\\,x<\\pi  \\\\ 5+2\\operatorname{cos}(x) & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge \\pi .\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}},$\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186233895 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2}{log}_{3}(x+7)\\cdot {log}_{3}(5-x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233896 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233896 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(-6x).$\n\n![](images/8a/8a4a1bc79f9d096c3ebe2b4ff37e8682f74ab04583101dfe1d7f1d5f2e8df8c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{log}_{4}(-6x)=\\infty \n$$"] [17592186233898 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}(x-2)^{2}+3x-5 & \\text{ for }\\,x<1 \\\\ sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}x)+2 & \\text{ for }\\,x\\ge 4.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,$\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186233904 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233904 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{4}(9x).$\n\n![](images/1e/1ead64d0b64a005122fa34c65677d5e7980c2a76245bbaca572325b8681ae958.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}{log}_{4}(9x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{log}_{4}(9x)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186233923 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=5,$ then\n\n$\\frac{10-f(0)}{(f(0))^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233929 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ and $f(3)=7,$ then\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}{(4{x}^{2}-5f(x))}^{3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233930 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)=-3,$ then\n\n${[(f(4))^{2}-11]}^{4}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186233940 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233940 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7):$\n\n![](images/37/371cbb841c67494626038fc93747a1d0a2b2245f422364911f1a859f4711868d.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{5}{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+7))=\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186233948 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}(x)+4\\operatorname{cot}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233953 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1):$ \n\n![](images/4d/4d81187377609f2cc79f2f972b1416a00f854d8c7a72731eb306aaa17d9f7339.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=2.\n$$"] [17592186233954 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}tan\\,(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}(x))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233955 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186233955 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1):$\n\n![](images/56/56e73e41a60732857504125b12eb3d3265b2633b0a9d1502058096725630793b.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(8\\operatorname{arctan}(3x-1))=(8\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=4\\pi .\n$$"] [17592186233960 :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$"] [17592186233961 :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right R